# Review is required on the paths where a merge means code execution, not
# content. A bad wiki page renders wrong and lint usually catches it; a bad
# script runs on the harness droplet with the API keys in its environment,
# and a bad workflow runs in CI with contents: write on this repo.
#
# Two maintainers are listed on every path, deliberately. GitHub does not
# let anyone approve their own pull request, so a single-owner rule on a repo
# where that owner writes most of the changes is not a review requirement —
# it is a lockout, and the way people get out of a lockout is by turning the
# rule off. Either one satisfies it. (Reduced from four on 2026-09-25 at the
# maintainer's request: the other two need not review wiki work. With two,
# the one who did not open a PR is its only possible approver.)
#
# The 3,700 content pages are NOT covered. They change hundreds at a time in
# normal enrichment batches, and requiring review there would make the rule
# noise that gets clicked through — which is how a rule stops protecting the
# paths that actually matter.

/scripts/               @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
/deploy/                @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
/.github/               @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
/mkdocs.yml             @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
/requirements-docs.txt  @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
/CLAUDE.md              @dsporcaro @MarkPotterLDA
